118. Pascal's Triangle (Easy)



Given an integer numRows, return the first numRows of Pascal's triangle.

In Pascal's triangle, each number is the sum of the two numbers directly above it as shown:

 

Example 1:

Input: numRows = 5
Output: [[1],[1,1],[1,2,1],[1,3,3,1],[1,4,6,4,1]]

Example 2:

Input: numRows = 1
Output: [[1]]

 

Constraints:

    1 <= numRows <= 30




Test Case:
Input
numRows =
5
Output
[[1],[1,1],[1,2,1],[1,3,3,1],[1,4,6,4,1]]



Answer:
class Solution {

    public List<List<Integer>> generate(int numRows) {

        // Puri Pascal's Triangle store karne ke liye
        List<List<Integer>> triangle = new ArrayList<>();

        // Har row ko ek-ek karke banayenge
        for (int i = 0; i < numRows; i++) {

            // Current row banane ke liye
            List<Integer> row = new ArrayList<>();

            // Har row ka first element hamesha 1 hota hai
            row.add(1);

            // Beech ke elements calculate karenge
            // First aur last element ko manually 1 rakhenge
            for (int j = 1; j < i; j++) {

                // Previous row nikal lo
                List<Integer> previousRow = triangle.get(i - 1);

                // Current element =
                // previous row ke do adjacent elements ka sum
                int value = previousRow.get(j - 1)
                           + previousRow.get(j);

                row.add(value);
            }

            // Agar row first row nahi hai
            // toh last mein 1 add kar do
            if (i > 0) {
                row.add(1);
            }

            // Current row ko triangle mein add kar do
            triangle.add(row);
        }

        return triangle;
    }
}



Time Complexity: O(numRows²)



Explanation:
Pascal's Triangle ko samajhna kaafi simple hai.

Har row:

First element = 1
Last element = 1
Beech ke elements = upar wali row ke do adjacent elements ka sum

